From 2222222222222222222222222222222222222222 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ASRock Rack <noreply@asrock.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] redfish: AMI Redfish Host Interface routes for X570D4I-2T

The stock AMI BIOS on this board ships a complete Redfish client (RedfishHi +
FirmwareConfigDrv + AmiRedfishDynExt). Given a service that speaks its dialect
on the USB host-interface link, an UNMODIFIED BIOS publishes its own attribute
registry and current BIOS settings and pulls staged writes back -- replacing the
injected BiosCfgOobDxe/SmbiosBmcPushDxe DXE drivers entirely.

AMI's protocol is not standard Redfish (POST to /Bios, GET/DELETE on Actions/*,
plus /bios/defaultSD, /BiosStaticFiles, /ami/static-file and
/DynamicExtension/RedfishExtensions), so those methods are merged onto the
existing Bios rules and the rest added as new routes. Every URI and verb was
extracted from the stock BIOS binaries; see
asrock-bmc/.claude/rhi-http-contract.md.

Note bmcweb's router tries the <str> parameter branch before static siblings
(findHelper in http/routing/trie.hpp), so a static /Systems/Self/Bios/ route can
never win against upstream's /Systems/<str>/Bios/. That is why the AMI methods
live on upstream's rule rather than on parallel routes.

The routes are unauthenticated by design, mirroring stock MegaRAC which seeds
HostInterfaces:<hi>:AuthNoneRoleId = "Administrator". Containment is a single
point in the authentication layer: Request now carries the address the
connection was accepted ON, and http_connection.hpp skips the 401 only for
connections whose local address is the host-interface address AND whose peer is
on the same link-local /16. Both halves are required -- bmcweb listens on
[::]:443 so endpoints arrive IPv4-mapped and must be normalised, and Linux's
weak host model otherwise lets any management-network client reach the
host-interface address by adding a route.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [board-specific; AMI-proprietary protocol]
---
